Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero window session storage

The session manager is original Zero, integrated in the terminal thread.
It does not create a native thread or a filesystem cache for each window.
Commands still complete through the one shared command/IO state machine.

Memory ownership
The loader owns the pool described by handoff+112/base and+120/size.
The two existing worker stacks use base..base+0x28000. Sessions start at
base+0x30000 and stop before base+size-0x20000, retaining32KiB separation
below and128KiB for the main stack/64KiB arena above. Pool address arithmetic,
alignment, minimum one-slot size and the IA32 address ceiling are checked.
With8192-byte slots the existing1MiB UEFI pool permits88 windows; the3MiB
BIOS pool permits344. Smaller valid pools naturally permit fewer windows.
An unusable pool leaves the existing single-terminal input buffer intact.
Unused slot headers are cleared at initialization; every newly opened slot
is fully zeroed before publishing its alive flag. No user data disk is used.

Shared u64 fields relative to console:
2048 slot base;2056 capacity;2064 focused index;2072 live count;
2080 layout0tile/1scroll;2088 layout generation;
2096 pending action;2104 requested numeric focus index;2112 focused pointer.
Actions:1new,2close,3next,4previous,5toggle,6tile,7scroll,8numeric focus.
Numeric targets are zero-based slot indices; displayed IDs are index+1.

Each8192-byte slot contains these u64 fields:
0alive,8ID,16ring next-write position,24ring byte count(max7168),
32input length,40overflow flag,48reserved,56selected volume,
64dirty generation,72drawn generation,144prompt initialized.
Input has512 bytes at+256; the7168-byte output ring begins at+1024.
Renderer-owned geometry occupies the remaining agreed header fields.

Switching is processed only while shell state10 is waiting for input.
The parser's mutable argument pointers and active filesystem requests are
never changed midway. The selected volume and incomplete command survive
focus changes. Keyboard modifiers, PS/2 prefix state, COM1 stream state and
ignore_lf remain global. A CRLF split across focus/new must consume its LF
without submitting the destination window's unfinished command. New windows
inherit the old selected volume. A first focus
requests a fresh prompt via state9; subsequent focus keeps the saved input.
Initial slot0 already owns the startup output; initial slot1 receives its
first prompt when focused. The last terminal cannot be closed.

Successful focus emits no message into unfinished input. A refused request
prints its diagnostic then reconstructs the prompt and saved input without
resetting the command. Error text plus480 input bytes fits the existing
2044-byte listing scratch. Focus/layout/create/close increment the global
layout generation; the renderer must check this after yielding to avoid
painting a closed/reused slot or a stale layout.

Focused hosted verification
The current own Zero compiler compiled a session harness with50 static
assertions, including saving/restoring unfinished input and overflow state,
volume independence, silent focus, first prompts, reuse after close, layout
changes, invalid numeric focus, all88 UEFI-sized slots, full-capacity refusal,
closing back to one terminal and last-close refusal. Guard bytes below the
session region and in the top128KiB main-stack reservation remained intact.
Eight additional initialization scenarios passed:3MiB capacity344, minimum
one-slot capacity, just-too-small pool, misalignment, u64 wrap, IA32 ceiling,
zero base and missing handoff. A further20 assertions cover global CRLF
framing across focus and new-window requests, including an unfinished
destination command. These hosted checks do not replace VM boot,
framebuffer, keyboard or filesystem integration tests.
Reproduce with bash review-tests/wm-sessions/run.sh; current logs and tested
source/compiler hashes are in verification/wm-sessions-0.8.0.
